    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cellular protein homeostasis (proteostasis) is maintained by a complex, adaptive proteostasis network (PN) composed of tightly regulated synthesis, quality control, and clearance systems. Disturbances in the PN that result in the misfolding and subsequent aggregation of proteins are implicated in many neurodegenerative disorders (NDs) such as Huntington’s, Alzheimer’s, and Parkinson’s diseases. While the PN’s late-stage responses to aggregation such as transcriptional changes and deposition of aggregated proteins have been well-documented, there remains a substantial gap in our understanding of the immediate responses of the PN to cytosolic protein aggregation. Elucidating these early responses to aggregation is essential to a comprehensive understanding of ND pathogenesis and can offer insight into previously unknown, potentially therapeutically tractable targets. Previous investigations into the earliest actions of the PN in response to acute shifts in protein solubility were limited by stochastic onset of aggregation in cells overexpressing folding-defective proteins. These models also lacked the synchronicity in aggregation necessary to achieve a temporal resolution capable of characterizing the PN response in the minutes following the onset of protein misfolding. Of particular interest is whether the rapid modulation of protein synthesis, a common fast-acting response to severe cellular stressors that mitigates insults to homeostasis, makes up part of the PN’s earliest response to the aggregation of a single protein in the absence of global stressors. To probe this question, this study used a novel system of inducible, widespread, synchronous aggregation of a single protein in cells, coupled with metabolic protein labeling and click chemistry, to investigate the immediate impact of protein aggregation on protein synthesis rate. The results showed that induced aggregation in our model system produced no significant change in global protein synthesis rate. Furthermore, induced aggregation did not increase phosphorylation of eIF2α, which mediates translational suppression in other models of aggregation.",
